WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.840
TRACE retrieves objects from clutter under arm occlusion.

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:07.840
Withdrawing the arm restores visibility, but interrupts manipulation.

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:10.840
The teacher plans once in simulation.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:14.840
The plan-conditioned student uses memory and partial feedback.

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:17.840
Pushing continues without sensing retractions.

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:21.840
TRACE achieves ninety percent success in hardware trials.

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:24.840
Pushing needs no online search or simulation.

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:28.840
The plan stays fixed; actions adapt as objects move.

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:29.840
Demonstrations.

00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:35.873
TRACE uses one privileged rollout to guide feedback control under self occlusion.

00:00:35.873 --> 00:00:39.622
A complete initial observation initializes the digital twin,

00:00:39.622 --> 00:00:44.307
and the teacher produces a nominal plan before manipulation begins.

00:00:44.307 --> 00:00:48.525
During pushing, the recurrent student combines partial object observations,

00:00:48.525 --> 00:00:54.148
robot proprioception, memory, and local rollout context to select its next action.

00:00:54.148 --> 00:01:00.708
The nominal plan stays fixed, while the executed actions adapt to the observed scene.

00:01:00.708 --> 00:01:04.925
As contact moves objects away from their predicted positions,

00:01:04.925 --> 00:01:08.205
the controller can depart from the plan.

00:01:08.205 --> 00:01:14.297
The observation history records object visibility and the age of the last observation,

00:01:14.297 --> 00:01:18.045
retaining context when the arm blocks the view.

00:01:18.045 --> 00:01:24.137
No online teacher queries, simulator rollouts, or sensing retractions are required during pushing.

00:01:24.137 --> 00:01:27.417
The rollout defines the scene-specific execution horizon.

00:01:27.417 --> 00:01:32.103
The arm withdraws for the final graspability check before retrieval.

00:01:32.103 --> 00:01:37.726
This separates closed-loop pushing under partial visibility from the final check that

00:01:37.726 --> 00:01:39.600
requires a clear view.

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:42.785
Here is TRACE across additional cluttered
scenes.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:49.297
Each scene begins with its own teacher
generated plan. The

00:01:49.297 --> 00:01:52.714
student adapts its actions using partial
observations and memory.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:55.276
The initial plan stays fixed, while

00:01:55.276 --> 00:01:57.551
execution responds to the changing scene.

00:01:58.250 --> 00:02:00.643
Across forty hardware trials, TRACE

00:02:00.643 --> 00:02:03.036
achieves ninety percent retrieval success.

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:07.145
No workspace violations or sensing
retractions

00:02:07.145 --> 00:02:10.231
during pushing were observed in these
trials.

00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:13.705
Its remaining failures reach the execution
budget.

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:19.565
Now, the baselines. Teacher Replay follows
a fixed plan without

00:02:19.565 --> 00:02:23.448
object feedback, so it cannot correct
contact induced deviations.

00:02:23.750 --> 00:02:27.792
The Online Teacher updates its actions
from complete scene

00:02:27.792 --> 00:02:31.833
observations, but repeatedly retracts the
arm to restore visibility.

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:36.243
P M B S uses tree search and batched

00:02:36.243 --> 00:02:39.986
simulation. Repeated planning and scene
reacquisition increase total time.

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:44.431
Spiral uses target centered pushing. It is
fastest

00:02:44.431 --> 00:02:47.612
on average, but less reliable in tight
clutter.

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:51.928
TRACE is two point nine times faster end
to end,

00:02:51.928 --> 00:02:55.606
with ninety percent success versus the
Online Teacher's ninety five.

00:02:57.100 --> 00:02:58.840
Plan conditioned retrieval.
